 agent agent   2337 Nov 11 11:40 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  37688 Nov 11 11:40 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_114006.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  27291 Nov 11 11:40 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 11:40 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    280 Nov 11 11:40 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2919 Nov 11 11:42 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   8260 Nov 11 11:42 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  25045 Nov 11 11:42 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 11:40 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 11:44 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    272 Nov 11 11:44 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      7 Nov 11 11:40 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, single-column)\n\n**Status: ready.** Edit mode is not active (no prior manuscript or staged inputs); `downstream_study` is absent from `paper_data.json`.\n\n## Provisional title\nRecalibrating the Control Variate in Prediction-Powered Mean Intervals: Small, Regime-Dependent Width Gains over PPI++ with Fixed Labels, Unlabeled Data, and Predictor\n\n## Central question\nWith labeled set, unlabeled set and predictor all fixed, can a cross-fitted recalibration g(Yhat), added as an extra control variate nested around PPI++, shorten a valid mean interval? By how much, and where does it help or fail?\n\n## Central thesis\nRecalibration gives a small, regime-dependent narrowing over PPI++ (final aggregate mean log width ratio 0.022, about 2% in geometric-mean width; 0.070 vs PPI lambda=1; 0.358 vs classical). Gains are concentrated where the predictor is miscalibrated (census_income 0.029, plankton 0.036, synthetic 0.041) and near zero for census_healthcare (0.0018) and census_education (0.0023). Coverage passed the evaluator gate but its point estimates (pooled 0.934, minimum family 0.91) lie below 0.95.\n\n## Qualification status (from `final.qualification.*`)\noutcome = **qualified**, status = completed, correct = true, claim_status_code = 2 (narrower than PPI++, coverage not rejected), controls passed, 0 failed episodes. This supports a conditional-support paper. It does not support generalization, superiority beyond the measured pools, or \"nominal coverage achieved\". `candidate_diagnostics_trust` is \"unverified\". The bootstrap lower bound is not in the artifacts. No ablation artifacts exist.\n\n## Why this framing\n- A headline \"beats PPI++\" would overstate a ~2% gain with below-nominal point coverage.\n- The label-free spline-basis route scored lowest in search (0.0046), and stacked calibration (0.0223) did not beat the simpler winner (0.0271). Neither is a paper contribution; both are a design-space contrast.\n- The efficiency-bound reading (gain = Var(Y - lambda*Yhat) → residual variance of the best g, bounded by Var(Y - E[Y|Yhat])) explains both when gains are possible and when they are not.\n- Search and final are kept separate. All 3 search attempts were scored (no unscored ones), each evaluated once on search pools disjoint from the final pools. The frozen winner was evaluated once on sealed pools.\n- Rejected framings: method-wins headline; stacked or label-free variant as the contribution.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Baselines: classical, PPI (lambda=1), PPI++ (primary comparator and fallback).\n2. Proposed: cross-fitted recalibrated PPI (isotonic and binned g; [Yhat, g] control variates; Student-t; adopt only if half-width ≥1% below PPI++).\n3. Supporting: stratified PPI (implemented, no reported metrics; do not give numbers).\n4. Design-contrast variants (single evaluation, search pools): label-free basis with PRESS, and NNLS-stacked calibration.\n\n## Results focus\n1. Sealed final per-family width reductions vs PPI++ and aggregate vs three comparators (derived percentages labeled as derived).\n2. Validity: pooled 0.934, min family 0.91, controls, failed episodes. State the gate is non-rejection.\n3. Where gain exists and where it does not. Mechanisms stay hypotheses without ablations.\n4. Spread across the five evaluator partitions (scores 0.0203–0.0247). These are not independent confidence intervals.\n5. Search-stage design contrast with per-family log ratios; single evaluations, no uncertainty.\n6. Analytic headroom section with assumptions and no-gain conditions. No numeric theory-vs-observed check is available, so state that it was not performed.\n\n## Figures (ICLR single-column, text width ≈5.5 in; all full-width; PDF + 300-DPI PNG from source)\n- **Fig. 1** `overview_setting_and_method`: diagram, generate-concept-figure, early (page 1–2). Shows fixed inputs → classical / PPI++ / recalibrated PPI with fallback → coverage and width ratio evaluation, with a headroom annotation.\n- **Fig. 2** `final_width_ratio`: matplotlib, 2 panels (per-family bars; aggregate vs 3 comparators). Coverage goes in Table 1.\n- **Fig. 3** `search_variant_contrast`: matplotlib, 1 panel, 5 families × 3 variants, labeled single evaluation.\n- **Tables:** Table 1 final summary; Table 2 estimator comparison and assumptions.\n- No trajectory or progression views.\n\n## Body-page use (8–9 pages)\nProblem and fixed-input setting; related work (PPI, PPI++, cross-PPI, stratified PPI, plus a few genuinely related bib entries); nested estimator and analytic headroom; evaluation protocol; final results with Table 1 and Fig. 2; search-stage design contrast; no-gain regimes and limitations. The limitations cover below-nominal coverage point estimates, mean estimand only, n=500 on real pools, no ablations, no n sweep, single sealed run, unverified diagnostics, and the in-population truth definition.\n\n## Supplementary plan\nEstimator hyperparameters from the frozen source; evaluation protocol and gates; full final tables; search-variant details; variance derivation; reproducibility and LLM Usage (venue-required).\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: Search Campaign, Iteration History, Progression, Milestones, Cycle N, Experiment A/B. Phrases: pipeline-native metric names, \"state of the art\", \"achieves nominal coverage\", \"significantly outperforms\".\n\n## Writer notes\n- Cite only keys in `references.bib`. DML, isotonic and control-variate sources are absent, so no citation unless retrieved later.\n- Anonymize the submission.\n- Use the reader-facing names \"recalibrated PPI\" and \"evaluation partitions\".\nfigures:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2938f847-5226-44ac-88d9-3502f088dfe2","uuid":"c1be908a-2651-4239-ab00-6f857fca398b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T11:45:04.884Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 700\ndrwx------ 5 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 11:45 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 11:40 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    751 Nov 11 11:40 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 11:40 .claude\nprw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 11:45 .paper_stream_20261111_114006.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 11:40 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3685 Nov 11 11:44 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 11:40 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 11:40 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   9041 Nov 11 11:40 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   4854 Nov 11 11:40 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    832 Nov 11 11:44 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  26973 Nov 10 13:47 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   8934 Nov 10 13:47 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 11:45 paper_agent_claude_20261111_114006.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  35013 Nov 11 11:40 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 11:40 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 11:40 paper_diagnostics_20261111_114006_170663.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2739 Nov 11 11:44 paper_events_20261111_114006.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    795 Nov 11 11:40 paper_prep_20261111_114006.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  62347 Nov 11 11:44 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  20323 Nov 11 11:44 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r