stematic review and meta-analysis},\n@Article{Khelfaoui2025BeyondJC,\n booktitle = {Frontiers in Microbiology},\n title = {Beyond just correlation: causal machine learning for the microbiome, from prediction to health policy with econometric tools},\n@Article{Tran2025ProbabilisticAO,\n booktitle = {Scientific Reports},\n title = {Probabilistic analysis of active earth pressures in spatially variable soils using machine learning and confidence intervals},\n@Article{Cadei2025PredictionPoweredCI,\n booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\n title = {Prediction-Powered Causal Inferences},\n@Article{Hofer2024BayesianPI,\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n title = {Bayesian Prediction-Powered Inference},\n@Article{Kilian2025AnytimevalidBI,\n booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\n title = {Anytime-valid, Bayes-assisted,Prediction-Powered Inference},\n@Article{Li2025PredictionPoweredAS,\n booktitle = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n title = {Prediction-Powered Adaptive Shrinkage Estimation},\n@Article{Cortinovis2025FABPPIFA,\n booktitle = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n title = {FAB-PPI: Frequentist, Assisted by Bayes, Prediction-Powered Inference},\n@Article{Gu2024LocalPI,\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n title = {Local Prediction-Powered Inference},\n@Article{Gan2022InferenceFI,\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n title = {Inference for Interpretable Machine Learning: Fast, Model-Agnostic Confidence Intervals for Feature Importance},\n@Article{Pettersson2025DebiasingML,\n booktitle = {AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence},\n title = {Debiasing Machine Learning Predictions for Causal Inference Without Additional Ground Truth Data: \"One Map, Many Trials\" in Satellite-Driven Poverty Analysis},\n@Article{Luo2024FederatedPI,\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n title = {Federated Prediction-Powered Inference from Decentralized Data},\n@Article{Huang2025FromPT,\n booktitle = {Symposium on Advances in Approximate Bayesian Inference},\n title = {From predictions to confidence intervals: an empirical study of conformal prediction methods for in-context learning},\n@Article{Fannjiang2025ReliableAS,\n booktitle = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n title = {Reliable algorithm selection for machine learning-guided design},\n@Article{Song2025SparseEA,\n booktitle = {Statistics and computing},\n title = {Sparse estimation and inference for prediction-powered semi-supervised linear regression},\n{\n \"status\": \"ready\",\n \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n \"recommended_title\": \"Recalibrating the Control Variate in Prediction-Powered Mean Intervals: Small, Regime-Dependent Width Gains over PPI++ with Fixed Labels, Unlabeled Data, and Predictor\",\n \"central_question\": \"When the labeled set, unlabeled set and predictor are all fixed, can a cross-fitted recalibration of the predictor output (added as a second control variate to PPI++) shorten a valid confidence interval for a population mean, by how much, and in which regimes does it help or fail?\",\n \"central_thesis\": \"Adding a cross-fitted monotone/binned recalibration g(Yhat) as an extra control variate nested around PPI++ gives a small but measurable average narrowing of mean intervals (about 2% in geometric-mean width on held-out sealed pools, ~7% vs PPI lambda=1, ~30% vs classical), concentrated where the predictor is miscalibrated (census income, plankton, miscalibrated synthetic) and near zero where the raw prediction is already well calibrated, with empirical coverage held at a level the evaluator's exact binomial tests did not reject but whose point estimates (pooled 0.934, minimum family 0.91) sit below the nominal 0.95.\",\n \"narrative_rationale\": \"The completed final evaluation is outcome=qualified (final.qualification.outcome), status=completed, correct=true, claim_status_code=2 (narrower than PPI++ with coverage not rejected, by a paired bootstrap lower bound on the aggregate score), so a conditional-support paper is grounded. The task itself welcomes conditional answers, and the strongest honest story is a bounded one: the PPI++ interval is not the shortest valid interval, but the headroom reachable with only (Y, Yhat) on the fixed inputs is small and regime dependent, which matches the efficiency-bound view (gain = drop from Var(Y - lambda*Yhat) to Var(Y - E[Y|Yhat])). Search and final are kept separate: three model variants were all scored in search (no unscored attempts), the frozen one was evaluated once on a disjoint sealed set of pools. Coverage point estimates below 0.95 and the absence of ablations/CIs on width ratios are central limitations and must be foregrounded, not hidden. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data, so none is used.\",\n \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Edit mode is not active (no previous_paper, no edit context files); a fresh framing was chosen. Candidate framings compared: (a) 'new method beats PPI++' headline - rejected because gains are ~2% and coverage is below nominal in point estimate; (b) 'label-free spline basis / stacked calibration as the contribution' - rejected because those variants scored lower or comparable in search and the stacked variant did not beat the simpler one; (c) conditional/limits paper centred on recalibrated control variates plus an efficiency-bound analysis of when gain is possible - selected.\",\n \"staged_input_impact\": [\n  \"No staged prior manuscript, new-program or old-program inputs are present; framing derives only from paper_data.json final_evaluation, the three scored search attempts, the task contract/evaluators and the frozen program source.\"\n ],\n \"method_groups\": [\n  {\n   \"name\": \"Classical labeled-only interval and PPI (lambda=1)\",\n   \"role\": \"baseline\",\n   \"include_reason\": \"Reference points that the evaluator reports directly (final mean log width ratio 0.358 vs classical and 0.070 vs PPI lambda=1, positive = narrower); show how much of the total gain over labeled-only inference is already captured by existing PPI machinery.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"name\": \"PPI++ (power-tuned scalar lambda on raw Yhat)\",\n   \"role\": \"baseline\",\n   \"include_reason\": \"The primary comparator for every width ratio and the score; also the fallback nested inside the proposed interval.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"name\": \"Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI (isotonic and quantile-binned g(Yhat); [Yhat, g] as control variates with variance-minimising weights; Student-t critical value; adopt the recalibrated interval only if its half-width is at least 1% below PPI++)\",\n   \"role\": \"proposed\",\n   \"include_reason\": \"The frozen search winner and the method evaluated in the sealed final; the paper's studied object.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"name\": \"Stratified PPI (quantile bins of Yhat, lambda=1 per stratum)\",\n   \"role\": \"supporting\",\n   \"include_reason\": \"Implemented in the frozen program as a comparison estimator and relevant prior work; include only as a method description/related work unless evaluator-reported numbers exist for it (none appear in paper_data: do not report numeric results for it).\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"name\": \"Search-stage alternative variants: label-free hinge-basis control variates with PRESS selection; repeated cross-fitted NNLS-stacked calibration\",\n   \"role\": \"ablation\",\n   \"include_reason\": \"Two scored alternative designs give a design-space contrast (search combined scores 0.0046 and 0.0223 vs 0.0271 for the winner). Present as a single-evaluation design comparison on search pools, not as controlled ablations, and without uncertainty claims.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"results_focus\": [\n  \"Sealed final evaluation (5 families, each 100 independent labeled/unlabeled draws, n=500, N=20000 for the four real pools; synthetic mixes four regimes, n in {200,500}, N in {2000,20000}): per-family log width ratio vs PPI++ (census_income 0.0287, census_healthcare 0.0018, census_education 0.0023, plankton 0.0359, synthetic 0.0413), aggregate 0.0220; convert to width reductions (1-exp(-s)) as derived values and say so.\",\n  \"Aggregate narrowing vs the three comparators: 0.022 (PPI++), 0.070 (PPI lambda=1), 0.358 (classical) mean log ratios; make clear that most of the gain over labeled-only inference comes from PPI itself and recalibration adds a small increment.\",\n  \"Validity reporting: pooled coverage 0.934 and minimum-family coverage 0.91 vs nominal 0.95; evaluator gate (exact one-sided binomial tests, pooled level 0.01, per-family Bonferroni) not rejected, controls (constant predictor, permuted predictor, shift) passed, 0 failed episodes. State that the point estimates are below nominal and the gate is a non-rejection, not proof of nominal coverage; per-family coverage other than the minimum is not available.\",\n  \"Where gain exists vs not: gains largest on synthetic (nonlinear miscalibrated regime is the designed case), plankton and census_income; near zero on census_healthcare and census_education. Do not attribute causes beyond what the analytic argument and predictor-calibration reasoning support; no per-regime ablation was run, so mechanisms are hypotheses unless a derivation backs them.\",\n  \"Stability across five evaluator partitions of the final run (partition scores 0.0203, 0.0213, 0.0220, 0.0235, 0.0247; derived range only; these are reset-seed partitions of one evaluator protocol, not independent method trainings, and candidate diagnostics trust is 'unverified'); report as spread, not as a confidence interval.\",\n  \"Search-stage design contrast on disjoint search pools (galaxies, forest, gene_expression, alphafold, synthetic; scored attempts 3 of 3, none unscored): winner 0.0271, stacked-calibration 0.0223, label-free basis 0.0046; per-family log ratios vs PPI++ for the winner: alphafold 0.0104, forest 0.0168, galaxies 0.0035, gene_expression 0.0290, synthetic 0.0757. Search coverage pooled 0.943 / min family 0.933 for the winner; the stacked variant had min family 0.917. Label the contrast as single evaluations; separate it clearly from final results.\",\n  \"Analytic section: asymptotic variance with control variates [Yhat, g]; PPI++ is nested (lambda on Yhat only), recalibrated gain governed by reduction from Var(Y - lambda*Yhat) to residual variance of the best g, bounded below by Var(Y - E[Y|Yhat]) when only Yhat is used; list assumptions (iid labeled/unlabeled from one population, fixed predictor, mean estimand, alpha=0.05, finite second moments, cross-fitted g at adequate rate) and no-gain conditions (already-linear calibration, uninformative predictor, small n). State explicitly that a numeric theory-vs-observed width-ratio check is not part of the available artifacts and was not performed.\"\n ],\n \"figure_plan\": [\n  \"Fig. 1 (early, page 1-2): conceptual overview - fixed inputs (n labeled pairs, N unlabeled predictions, frozen predictor) -> three interval constructions on the same data (classical; PPI++ with scalar lambda on Yhat; recalibrated PPI with cross-fitted g(Yhat) as an additional control variate and a 1%-margin fallback to PPI++) -> evaluation by coverage and width ratio vs PPI++; annotate the efficiency-bound idea (headroom = Var(Y-lambda*Yhat) down to Var(Y-E[Y|Yhat])).\",\n  \"Fig. 2: sealed final results - panel (a) per-family width reduction vs PPI++ (5 bars, positive = narrower); panel (b) aggregate mean log ratio against PPI++, PPI lambda=1, classical (3 bars). Coverage goes in a small table next to it, not a figure.\",\n  \"Fig. 3: search-stage design contrast - grouped bars of per-family log width ratio vs PPI++ for the three model variants across five search families, with the coverage-minimum shown in a table or annotation; explicitly 'single evaluation per variant'.\",\n  \"Table 1: final summary (per-family log ratio and derived width ratio; pooled and min coverage; controls; failed episodes; partition score range). Table 2: method/estimator comparison (what each interval uses, assumptions).\"\n ],\n \"figure_specs\": [\n  {\n   \"figure_id\": \"overview_setting_and_method\",\n   \"required\": true,\n   \"purpose\": \"Glance-level view of the fixed-input problem, the nested interval constructions, and the evaluation protocol, so the reader sees what is being shortened and relative to what.\",\n   \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n   \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n   \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n   \"metric\": \"none (conceptual)\",\n   \"split_by\": \"none\",\n   \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n   \"target_width_fraction\": 0.95,\n   \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR template is single-column (text width about 5.5 in); a left-to-right schematic with 3 stages and short labels needs the full text width to keep labels at >= 8 pt; place on page 1 or 2.\",\n   \"allow_progression\": false,\n   \"max_panels\": 1,\n   \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n   \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n  },\n  {\n   \"figure_id\": \"final_width_ratio\",\n   \"required\": true,\n   \"purpose\": \"Show the sealed-final width narrowing vs PPI++ by family and vs the three comparators in aggregate, with the zero-gain line visible.\",\n   \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n   \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n   \"group_by\": \"family\",\n   \"metric\": \"log width ratio vs comparator (positive = narrower), plus derived percent reduction\",\n   \"split_by\": \"panel (per-family vs aggregate)\",\n   \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n   \"target_width_fraction\": 0.95,\n   \"layout_rationale\": \"Two side-by-side panels (5 bars and 3 bars) with family-name tick labels; full text width keeps tick labels horizontal and legible; do not shrink to column width.\",\n   \"allow_progression\": false,\n   \"max_panels\": 2,\n   \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n   \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"figure_id\": \"search_variant_contrast\",\n   \"required\": true,\n   \"purpose\": \"Contrast the three scored model variants on search pools to show which design choices (monotone/binned recalibration vs smooth label-free basis vs stacked calibration) carry the gain; kept separate from final results.\",\n   \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n   \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n   \"group_by\": \"search_family\",\n   \"metric\": \"log width ratio vs PPI++ (positive = narrower)\",\n   \"split_by\": \"none\",\n   \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n   \"target_width_fraction\": 0.95,\n   \"layout_rationale\": \"5 categories x 3 series in one panel needs full text width for readable legends; single panel avoids density problems.\",\n   \"allow_progression\": false,\n   \"max_panels\": 1,\n   \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n   \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n  }\n ],\n \"supplementary_plan\": {\n  \"purpose\": \"Provide reimplementation-level and pr\nresults/ideas:\n01_cross_fitted_recalibrated_ppi_intervals\n02_label_free_basis_ppi_with_press_selection\n03_repeated_cross_fitted_stacked_calibration_ppi_intervals\n\nresults/task:\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\ntask.md\nls: cannot access '<HOME>': No such file or directory","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2938f847-5226-44ac-88d9-3502f088dfe2","uuid":"cf49a569-6830-43b5-854f-093bbdb9d227","timestamp":"2026-11-11T11:45:15.419Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"@Article{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI,\n booktitle = {Science},\n title = {Prediction-powered inference},\n@Article{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP,\n booktitle = {Annals of Applied Statistics},\n title = {PPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference},\n@Article{Zrnic2023CrosspredictionpoweredI,\n booktitle = {Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America},\n title = {Cross-prediction-powered inference},\n@Article{Liu2024CreditRP,\n booktitle = {Journal of Forecasting},\n title = {Credit risk prediction based on causal machine learning: Bayesian network learning, default inference, and i