{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw71BBS3Ntc8ineNzu41","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016db2LK5XuzAdutvRvAAY4v","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\nanchor=\"\\\\subsection{Stability across evaluator partitions}\"\nadd=r\"\"\"\\emph{Regime-level headroom is much larger than the family-level average.} Within the nonlinear-miscalibrated synthetic regime alone, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the unlabeled sample then carries less of the variance. The family-level ceiling of 0.0416 is much smaller because three of the four regimes have no headroom and the evaluator's ratio of summed widths weights the nonlinear regime by its (smallest) interval width. For census income the same calculation gives at most 0.0034 over an unclipped linear weight, whatever $r$.% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n\\label{tab:rsens}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n & $(200,20000)$ & $(500,20000)$ & $(200,2000)$ & $(500,2000)$ \\\\\n$r=n/N$ & 0.010 & 0.025 & 0.100 & 0.250 \\\\\n\\midrule\nsynthetic, nonlinear regime & 0.2855 & 0.2750 & 0.2323 & 0.1776 \\\\\ncensus income & 0.0034 & 0.0034 & 0.0030 & 0.0025 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\"\"\"\nassert anchor in s\ns=s.replace(anchor, add+anchor,1)\nanchor2=\"\\\\paragraph{Practical reading.}\"\nadd2=r\"\"\"\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n\n\"\"\"\nassert anchor2 in s\ns=s.replace(anchor2, add2+anchor2,1)\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\n(pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/tmp/l1.log 2>&1; bibtex paper >/tmp/b.log 2>&1; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/tmp/l2.log 2>&1; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/tmp/l3.log 2>&1); grep \"^!\" /tmp/l3.log; /tmp/pyenv/bin/python - <<'E' 2>&1 | grep -v -i warn\nimport pymupdf,re\nd=pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\nprint(len(d))\nfor i,p in enumerate(d):\n    t=p.get_text()\n    bl=[b for b in p.get_text(\"blocks\") if b[3]<735 and b[1]>40 and not b[4].strip().isdigit() and 'Generated by' not in b[4]]\n    ymax=max([b[3] for b in bl if len(b[4])>20] or [0])\n    print(i+1, round(ymax), 'REFERENCES' in t)\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2051,"cache_read_input_tokens":209764,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2051},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2938f847-5226-44ac-88d9-3502f088dfe2","uuid":"f379727e-8e28-4d71-9bf5-b40b0b5d1f34","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:00:58.427Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw719etsyRYzSEZ5WiiR"}
